            <ArticleTitle>Usefulness of the evaluation of stent fracture by 64-multi-detector row computed tomography.</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>135-8</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <ELocationID EIdType="doi" ValidYN="Y">10.1016/j.jjcc.2007.11.003</ELocationID>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText>A 77-year-old man with unstable angina pectoris showed severe coronary stenosis with soft plaque in the left anterior descending coronary artery (LAD) as detected by 64-multi-detector row computed tomography (MDCT). Percutaneous coronary intervention (PCI) with a bare-metal stent to the LAD was performed, and stent fracture immediately after PCI was clearly detected by MDCT. MDCT may be useful for identifying a fractured stent and for management after stent implantation.</AbstractText>
            </Abstract>
